You are tasked with implementing a simplified version of a message dispatching system in Rust. The system consists of a message queue and an application object. The application object has methods for dispatching messages and rendering. Your goal is to implement the `MessageQueue` and `Application` structs, along with their associated methods, to achieve the desired functionality.

The `MessageQueue` struct has a `pop` method that retrieves and removes the last message from the queue. The `Application` struct has `__dispatch` and `__render` methods for message dispatching and rendering, respectively. Additionally, the `Application` struct has a `ready` method that sets the readiness state of the application.

Your task is to complete the implementation of the `MessageQueue` and `Application` structs, along with their associated methods, to achieve the following functionality:
1. Continuously pop messages from the queue and dispatch them using the `__dispatch` method until the queue is empty.
2. After dispatching all messages, call the `__render` method to render the application.
3. Finally, set the readiness state of the application to true using the `ready` method.

You should use the provided code snippet as a reference to understand the usage of the methods and the overall flow of the system.

### Solution:
```rust
struct MessageQueue(Vec<String>);

impl MessageQueue {
    fn new() -> Self {
        MessageQueue(Vec::new())
    }

    fn pop(&mut self) -> Option<String> {
        self.0.pop()
    }
}

struct Application {
    ready: bool,
}

impl Application {
    fn new() -> Self {
        Application { ready: false }
    }

    fn __dispatch(&self, msg: String, _queue: &mut MessageQueue) {
        // Implementation for message dispatching
        println!("Dispatching message: {}", msg);
    }

    fn __render(&self, _queue: &mut MessageQueue) {
        // Implementation for rendering
        println!("Rendering application");
    }

    fn ready(&mut self, state: bool) {
        self.ready = state;
    }
}

unsafe {
    let mut queue = MessageQueue::new();
    queue.0.push("Message 1".to_string());
    queue.0.push("Message 2".to_string());
    queue.0.push("Message 3".to_string());

    let app = Application::new();

    while let Some(msg) = queue.pop() {
        app.__dispatch(msg, &mut queue);
        while let Some(msg) = queue.pop() {
            app.__dispatch(msg, &mut queue);
        }
        app.__render(&mut queue);
    }

    app.ready(true);
}
```

