### Solution:
```swift
class ReorderableList<T> {
    var items: [T]
    var isReorderable: Bool

    init(items: [T], isReorderable: Bool) {
        self.items = items
        self.isReorderable = isReorderable
    }

    func canMoveItem(at index: Int) -> Bool {
        return isReorderable && index >= 0 && index < items.count
    }

    func moveObject(from sourceIndex: Int, to destinationIndex: Int) {
        guard isReorderable, !items.isEmpty, sourceIndex >= 0, sourceIndex < items.count, destinationIndex >= 0, destinationIndex < items.count else {
            return
        }
        let item = items.remove(at: sourceIndex)
        items.insert(item, at: destinationIndex)
    }
}
```

In the `canMoveItem(at index: Int)` method, we check if the list is reorderable and if the index is within the bounds of the list.

In the `moveObject(from sourceIndex: Int, to destinationIndex: Int)` method, we first ensure that the list is reorderable, not empty, and that both the source and destination indices are valid. Then, we remove the item at the source index and insert it at the destination index within the list. If any of the conditions are not met, no action is taken.

