### Solution:
```java
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class CategoryCategorizer {
    private enum Category {
        K1(new BigDecimal("2.72"), new BigDecimal("12.04")),
        K2(new BigDecimal("12.05"), new BigDecimal("20.23")),
        K3(new BigDecimal("20.28"), new BigDecimal("32.42")),
        K4(new BigDecimal("32.44"), new BigDecimal("54.08")),
        K5(new BigDecimal("54.31"), new BigDecimal("100.21"));

        private final BigDecimal min;
        private final BigDecimal max;

        Category(BigDecimal min, BigDecimal max) {
            this.min = min;
            this.max = max;
        }

        public BigDecimal getMin() {
            return min;
        }

        public BigDecimal getMax() {
            return max;
        }
    }

    public static String findCategory(BigDecimal value) {
        for (Category category : Category.values()) {
            if (value.compareTo(category.getMin()) >= 0 && value.compareTo(category.getMax()) <= 0) {
                return category.name();
            }
        }
        return "Uncategorized";
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BigDecimal testValue1 = new BigDecimal("15.0");
        BigDecimal testValue2 = new BigDecimal("5.0");
        BigDecimal testValue3 = new BigDecimal("100.5");

        System.out.println("Category for " + testValue1 + ": " + findCategory(testValue1));
        System.out.println("Category for " + testValue2 + ": " + findCategory(testValue2));
        System.out.println("Category for " + testValue3 + ": " + findCategory(testValue3));
    }
}
```

The `findCategory` method iterates through the defined categories and checks if the input value falls within the range of each category. If the value falls within a category's range, the method returns the corresponding category name. If the value does not fall within any defined category, the method returns "Uncategorized". The main method demonstrates the usage of the `findCategory` method with sample test values.

