### Problem:
You are tasked with implementing a custom event system in a game development project. The game has various events that need to be handled, such as player damage, player effects, player authentication, player class changes, item pickups, item drops, and room cleaning activations. To achieve this, you need to create a flexible event system that allows for the registration of event handlers and the triggering of events.

Your task is to design a class or set of classes that can manage these events efficiently. The event system should support the registration of multiple event handlers for each type of event and provide a way to trigger the events, ensuring that all registered handlers are called when an event occurs.

Your implementation should demonstrate the ability to register event handlers, trigger events, and ensure that all registered handlers are properly invoked when an event is triggered.

Write a program in a language of your choice that showcases the implementation of the event system for the given events. Your program should include the necessary classes, methods, and data structures to manage the events and event handlers effectively.

### Solution:
```java
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

// Define the Event type enum
enum EventType {
    OnPlayerDamage,
    OnPlayerEffect,
    OnPlayerAuth,
    OnPlayerClassChange,
    OnItemPickup,
    OnItemDrop,
    OnCleanRoomActivate
}

// Define the Event class to hold event handlers
class Event {
    private Map<EventType, List<EventHandler>> eventHandlers;

    public Event() {
        eventHandlers = new HashMap<>();
        for (EventType eventType : EventType.values()) {
            eventHandlers.put(eventType, new ArrayList<>());
        }
    }

    public void registerEventHandler(EventType eventType, EventHandler handler) {
        eventHandlers.get(eventType).add(handler);
    }

    public void triggerEvent(EventType eventType) {
        for (EventHandler handler : eventHandlers.get(eventType)) {
            handler.handleEvent();
        }
    }
}

// Define the EventHandler interface
interface EventHandler {
    void handleEvent();
}

// Example implementation of event handlers
class PlayerDamageHandler implements EventHandler {
    @Override
    public void handleEvent() {
        System.out.println("Player damaged!");
    }
}

class ItemPickupHandler implements EventHandler {
    @Override
    public void handleEvent() {
        System.out.println("Item picked up!");
    }
}

// Main program to showcase the event system
public class EventSystemDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Event eventSystem = new Event();

        // Register event handlers
        eventSystem.registerEventHandler(EventType.OnPlayerDamage, new PlayerDamageHandler());
        eventSystem.registerEventHandler(EventType.OnItemPickup, new ItemPickupHandler());

        // Trigger events
        eventSystem.triggerEvent(EventType.OnPlayerDamage);
        eventSystem.triggerEvent(EventType.OnItemPickup);
    }
}
```

In this solution, we define an `Event` class to manage event handlers for different event types. The `Event` class uses a map to store event handlers for each event type and provides methods to register event handlers and trigger events. We also define an `EventHandler` interface that all event handlers must implement. The main program demonstrates the registration of event handlers and triggering of events for the given event types. When the events are triggered, all registered event handlers for that event type are properly invoked.

