    // Your implementation here
}
```

For example, given the following construct:
```
        A
       / \
      B   C
     / \   \
    D   E   F
```
If the starting node identifier is "A", the expected output of the `depthFirstSearch` method would be ["A", "B", "D", "E", "C", "F"].

### Solution:
```java
public List<String> depthFirstSearch(Node construct, String startNodeIdentifier) {
    List<String> visitedNodes = new ArrayList<>();
    Set<String> visitedSet = new HashSet<>();
    depthFirstSearchHelper(construct, startNodeIdentifier, visitedNodes, visitedSet);
    return visitedNodes;
}

private void depthFirstSearchHelper(Node node, String identifier, List<String> visitedNodes, Set<String> visitedSet) {
    if (node == null || visitedSet.contains(identifier)) {
        return;
    }
    visitedNodes.add(identifier);
    visitedSet.add(identifier);
    for (Node child : node.children) {
        depthFirstSearchHelper(child, child.identifier, visitedNodes, visitedSet);
    }
}
```

The `depthFirstSearch` method initializes an empty list to store the visited nodes and a set to keep track of visited identifiers. It then calls the `depthFirstSearchHelper` method to perform the actual depth-first search. The helper method recursively visits each child node in depth-first order and adds their identifiers to the visited list while avoiding revisiting already visited nodes.

