### Solution:
```csharp
using System;
using System.Runtime.InteropServices;

public class ResourceInjector : IDisposable
{
    private IntPtr _resourcePointer;
    private bool _isDisposed;

    public IntPtr Injector()
    {
        if (_isDisposed)
        {
            throw new ObjectDisposedException(nameof(ResourceInjector), "Resource has been disposed of");
        }
        return _resourcePointer;
    }

    public bool IsDisposed
    {
        get { return _isDisposed; }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Dispose(true);
        GC.SuppressFinalize(this);
    }

    protected virtual void Dispose(bool disposing)
    {
        if (!_isDisposed)
        {
            if (disposing)
            {
                // Release managed resources
            }
            // Release unmanaged resources
            _isDisposed = true;
        }
    }

    ~ResourceInjector()
    {
        Dispose(false);
    }
}
```
In the solution, the `ResourceInjector` class is implemented with a private field `_resourcePointer` to store the pointer to the injected resource and a private field `_isDisposed` to track whether the resource has been disposed of. The `Injector` method checks if the resource has been disposed of and throws an `ObjectDisposedException` if necessary. The `IsDisposed` property returns the value of `_isDisposed`. The class also implements the `IDisposable` interface for proper resource cleanup, with the `Dispose` method releasing both managed and unmanaged resources and setting `_isDisposed` to `true`. The finalizer calls the `Dispose` method to release unmanaged resources.

