You are tasked with implementing a simple metrics tracking system for a web application. The system should be able to track counters, gauges, and histograms for various metrics. You will need to create a MetricsTracker struct that provides methods for updating and retrieving the metrics.

Your task is to implement the MetricsTracker struct and its associated methods to track and retrieve metrics. The MetricsTracker should support the following functionalities:
- Increment a counter by a specified amount.
- Decrement a counter by a specified amount.
- Set the value of a gauge to a specified value.
- Record a value in a histogram.

You should also implement methods to retrieve the current value of a counter, gauge, or histogram.

You are provided with the following code snippet as a starting point:
```
use metrics::{Counter, Gauge, Histogram, Unit};

#[test]
fn test_basic_functionality() {
    // Your implementation of MetricsTracker will be used here to test its functionality
}
```

Implement the MetricsTracker struct and its associated methods to fulfill the requirements.

### Solution:
```rust
use std::collections::HashMap;

struct MetricsTracker {
    counters: HashMap<String, i32>,
    gauges: HashMap<String, i32>,
    histograms: HashMap<String, Vec<i32>>,
}

impl MetricsTracker {
    fn new() -> Self {
        MetricsTracker {
            counters: HashMap::new(),
            gauges: HashMap::new(),
            histograms: HashMap::new(),
        }
    }

    fn increment_counter(&mut self, name: &str, amount: i32) {
        let counter = self.counters.entry(name.to_string()).or_insert(0);
        *counter += amount;
    }

    fn decrement_counter(&mut self, name: &str, amount: i32) {
        let counter = self.counters.entry(name.to_string()).or_insert(0);
        *counter -= amount;
    }

    fn set_gauge(&mut self, name: &str, value: i32) {
        self.gauges.insert(name.to_string(), value);
    }

    fn record_histogram(&mut self, name: &str, value: i32) {
        let histogram = self.histograms.entry(name.to_string()).or_insert(Vec::new());
        histogram.push(value);
    }

    fn get_counter(&self, name: &str) -> Option<&i32> {
        self.counters.get(name)
    }

    fn get_gauge(&self, name: &str) -> Option<&i32> {
        self.gauges.get(name)
    }

    fn get_histogram(&self, name: &str) -> Option<&Vec<i32>> {
        self.histograms.get(name)
    }
}

#[test]
fn test_basic_functionality() {
    let mut tracker = MetricsTracker::new();

    tracker.increment_counter("requests", 1);
    tracker.increment_counter("requests", 3);
    tracker.decrement_counter("requests", 2);

    tracker.set_gauge("active_users", 10);

    tracker.record_histogram("response_times", 50);
    tracker.record_histogram("response_times", 60);
    tracker.record_histogram("response_times", 70);

    assert_eq!(*tracker.get_counter("requests").unwrap(), 2);
    assert_eq!(*tracker.get_gauge("active_users").unwrap(), 10);
    assert_eq!(*tracker.get_histogram("response_times").unwrap(), vec![50, 60, 70]);
}
```

In this solution, we define a MetricsTracker struct that uses HashMaps to store counters, gauges, and histograms. The struct provides methods to increment and decrement counters, set gauge values, and record values in histograms. Additionally, it includes methods to retrieve the current values of counters, gauges, and histograms. The provided test case demonstrates the basic functionality of the MetricsTracker by performing various metric tracking operations and asserting the expected results.

