You are tasked with implementing a function to standardize apart a first-order logic sentence in place. First-order logic sentences often contain quantified variables, and standardizing apart involves renaming variables to ensure that no two quantified variables have the same name. This is necessary to prevent unintended variable capture when performing logical inference.

You are provided with the following classes from the aima.core.logic.fol package:
- `aima.core.logic.fol.CollectAllVariables`: A class that collects all the variables in a first-order logic sentence.
- `aima.core.logic.fol.StandardizeApartInPlace`: A class that provides a method to standardize apart a first-order logic sentence in place.

Your task is to implement the `standardizeApart` method in the `StandardizeApartInPlace` class. The method should take a first-order logic sentence as input and standardize apart its variables in place, ensuring that no two quantified variables have the same name.

```java
package aima.core.logic.fol;

import java.util.Set;

public class StandardizeApartInPlace {

    public static void standardizeApart(FOLNode sentence) {
        Set<String> variables = CollectAllVariables.collectAllVariables(sentence);
        // Your implementation to standardize apart the variables in the given sentence
        // should go here
    }
}
```

You should ensure that the implementation correctly renames the variables in the input sentence to standardize them apart, following the standard conventions for variable renaming.

### Solution:
```java
package aima.core.logic.fol;

import java.util.Set;

public class StandardizeApartInPlace {

    private static int counter = 0;

    public static void standardizeApart(FOLNode sentence) {
        Set<String> variables = CollectAllVariables.collectAllVariables(sentence);
        for (String variable : variables) {
            if (variable.startsWith("X") || variable.startsWith("Y") || variable.startsWith("Z")) {
                continue; // Skip standard variables
            }
            String newVariable = generateNewVariable();
            renameVariableInSentence(sentence, variable, newVariable);
        }
    }

    private static String generateNewVariable() {
        return "X" + counter++;
    }

    private static void renameVariableInSentence(FOLNode sentence, String oldVariable, String newVariable) {
        if (sentence instanceof FOLQuantifiedSentence) {
            FOLQuantifiedSentence quantifiedSentence = (FOLQuantifiedSentence) sentence;
            if (quantifiedSentence.getVariables().contains(oldVariable)) {
                quantifiedSentence.getVariables().remove(oldVariable);
                quantifiedSentence.getVariables().add(newVariable);
            }
            renameVariableInSentence(quantifiedSentence.getQuantified(), oldVariable, newVariable);
        } else if (sentence instanceof FOLConnectiveSentence) {
            FOLConnectiveSentence connectiveSentence = (FOLConnectiveSentence) sentence;
            renameVariableInSentence(connectiveSentence.getFirst(), oldVariable, newVariable);
            renameVariableInSentence(connectiveSentence.getSecond(), oldVariable, newVariable);
        } else if (sentence instanceof FOLAtomicSentence) {
            FOLAtomicSentence atomicSentence = (FOLAtomicSentence) sentence;
            for (int i = 0; i < atomicSentence.getArgs().size(); i++) {
                if (atomicSentence.getArgs().get(i).equals(oldVariable)) {
                    atomicSentence.getArgs().set(i, newVariable);
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

In the solution, we maintain a counter to generate new variable names and use a recursive approach to traverse the first-order logic sentence and rename the variables as necessary. The `standardizeApart` method correctly standardizes apart the variables in the input sentence, ensuring that no two quantified variables have the same name.

