### Solution:
```rust
fn concat_strings(s1: Thunk<String>, s2: Thunk<String>) -> Thunk<String> {
    s1.bind(move |str1| {
        s2.bind(move |str2| {
            Thunk::pure(str1 + &str2)
        })
    })
}

fn main() {
    let hello = Thunk::from_value("Hello, ".to_string());
    let world = Thunk::from_value("world!".to_string());

    let lazy_concatenation = concat_strings(hello, world);
    // At this point, no evaluation has occurred

    println!("{}", lazy_concatenation.eval());
    // The concatenation is performed only when the result is explicitly requested
}
```

In this solution, the `concat_strings` function takes two Thunk instances containing strings and uses the `bind` method to lazily concatenate the strings. The `main` function demonstrates the lazy evaluation behavior by creating Thunk instances for "Hello, " and "world!", lazily concatenating them, and then explicitly evaluating the result when needed.

