You are tasked with implementing a simple version control system for a text editor. The system should support the following operations:

1. `validate()`: This method should return a boolean value indicating whether the current version of the text is valid or not. A version is considered valid if it has not been rolled back or reloaded since the last save operation.

2. `rollback()`: This method should revert the text to the last saved version. After rolling back, the version history should be updated accordingly.

3. `reload()`: This method should reload the text to the last saved version, discarding any changes made since the last save operation.

You are given the following interface for the version control system:

```typescript
interface VersionControl {
  validate(): boolean;
  rollback(): void;
  reload(): void;
}
```

Your task is to implement the `VersionControl` interface to create a simple version control system for the text editor.

Implement the `VersionControl` interface in a class called `TextEditorVersionControl`.

### Solution:
```typescript
class TextEditorVersionControl implements VersionControl {
  private versions: string[];
  private currentVersionIndex: number;

  constructor(initialText: string) {
    this.versions = [initialText];
    this.currentVersionIndex = 0;
  }

  validate(): boolean {
    return this.currentVersionIndex === this.versions.length - 1;
  }

  rollback(): void {
    if (this.currentVersionIndex > 0) {
      this.currentVersionIndex--;
    }
  }

  reload(): void {
    if (this.currentVersionIndex < this.versions.length - 1) {
      this.currentVersionIndex++;
    }
  }

  save(text: string): void {
    this.versions = this.versions.slice(0, this.currentVersionIndex + 1);
    this.versions.push(text);
    this.currentVersionIndex++;
  }

  getCurrentText(): string {
    return this.versions[this.currentVersionIndex];
  }
}
```

In this solution, we implement the `VersionControl` interface in the `TextEditorVersionControl` class. The class maintains an array of text versions and a pointer to the current version. The `validate` method checks if the current version is the latest one. The `rollback` and `reload` methods update the current version pointer accordingly. Additionally, we introduce a `save` method to save a new version of the text and update the version history. The `getCurrentText` method returns the text of the current version.

