public class PermissionManager {
    private Map<String, Map<String, ServicePermission>> filterPermissions = new HashMap<>();

    public void addPermission(ServicePermission sp) {
        if (sp.service != null) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("cannot add to collection: " + sp);
        }

        final String name = sp.getName();
        final Filter f = sp.filter;

        synchronized (this) {
            /* select the bucket for the permission */
            Map<String, ServicePermission> pc;
            if (f != null) {
                pc = filterPermissions.get(f.getName());
                if (pc == null) {
                    pc = new HashMap<>();
                    filterPermissions.put(f.getName(), pc);
                }
            } else {
                pc = filterPermissions.get(null);
                if (pc == null) {
                    pc = new HashMap<>();
                    filterPermissions.put(null, pc);
                }
            }
            pc.put(name, sp);
        }
    }
}
```

In the `addPermission` method, the code first checks if the service associated with the permission is null. If not, it throws an `IllegalArgumentException`. Then, it retrieves the name and filter associated with the permission. The method uses a synchronized block to ensure thread safety while adding the permission to the correct bucket based on the filter. If a filter is provided, it retrieves the corresponding bucket from `filterPermissions` or creates a new one if it doesn't exist. If no filter is provided, it uses a null key to store permissions without a filter. Finally, it adds the permission to the selected bucket. This implementation ensures thread safety and proper handling of filters while adding permissions to the system.

