### Problem:
You are tasked with implementing a URI parser interface in a programming language of your choice. The interface, `Parser`, contains a method `parseURI` that takes a string representing a URI and returns an object with parsed URI parts. The parsed URI parts should include the scheme, host, port, path, query parameters, and fragment.

Your task is to create a class that implements the `Parser` interface and provides a concrete implementation for the `parseURI` method. The input URI will be a string in the format "scheme://host:port/path?query#fragment". You need to parse this string and return an object containing the parsed parts of the URI.

For example, given the input URI "https://www.example.com:8080/path/to/resource?param1=value1&param2=value2#section", the expected parsed URI parts would be:
- Scheme: "https"
- Host: "www.example.com"
- Port: 8080
- Path: "/path/to/resource"
- Query Parameters: { "param1": "value1", "param2": "value2" }
- Fragment: "section"

Your implementation should handle various URI formats and edge cases, ensuring that the parsed URI parts are correctly extracted and returned in the object.

### Solution:
```java
import java.net.URI;
import java.net.URISyntaxException;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

interface Parser {
    ParsedURI parseURI(String uri);
}

class ParsedURI {
    private String scheme;
    private String host;
    private int port;
    private String path;
    private Map<String, String> queryParams;
    private String fragment;

    public ParsedURI(String scheme, String host, int port, String path, Map<String, String> queryParams, String fragment) {
        this.scheme = scheme;
        this.host = host;
        this.port = port;
        this.path = path;
        this.queryParams = queryParams;
        this.fragment = fragment;
    }

    // Getters for parsed URI parts
    // ...

}

class URIParser implements Parser {
    @Override
    public ParsedURI parseURI(String uri) {
        try {
            URI javaURI = new URI(uri);
            String scheme = javaURI.getScheme();
            String host = javaURI.getHost();
            int port = javaURI.getPort();
            String path = javaURI.getPath();
            String query = javaURI.getQuery();
            String fragment = javaURI.getFragment();

            Map<String, String> queryParams = new HashMap<>();
            if (query != null) {
                String[] pairs = query.split("&");
                for (String pair : pairs) {
                    String[] keyValue = pair.split("=");
                    if (keyValue.length == 2) {
                        queryParams.put(keyValue[0], keyValue[1]);
                    }
                }
            }

            return new ParsedURI(scheme, host, port, path, queryParams, fragment);
        } catch (URISyntaxException e) {
            // Handle URI parsing exception
            e.printStackTrace();
            return null;
        }
    }
}
```

In this solution, we define an interface `Parser` with a method `parseURI` that returns a `ParsedURI` object containing the parsed parts of the input URI. The `ParsedURI` class encapsulates the parsed URI parts and provides getters for accessing them.

The `URIParser` class implements the `Parser` interface and provides a concrete implementation for the `parseURI` method. It uses the `java.net.URI` class to parse the input URI and extract the scheme, host, port, path, query parameters, and fragment. The query parameters are parsed into a map for easy access.

The implementation handles URI parsing exceptions and returns `null` in case of an error. This solution provides a robust and accurate parsing of URI parts, handling various URI formats and edge cases.

